# Sticky workload knobs for scripts/run-suite.sh.
#
# Everything here is COMMENTED OUT on purpose. `k6/lib/config.js` on each variant branch is
# the single source of truth for defaults; this file only exists so you can pin a knob
# across a whole campaign instead of retyping it on every invocation. Uncommenting a line
# makes it sticky; leaving it commented means k6's own default applies.
#
# That asymmetry is deliberate. Writing the defaults out here as live assignments would
# create a second place they are defined, and the moment k6's default moved the two would
# disagree silently — the same trap REPLICAS/EXPECTED_REPLICAS already caused once.
#
# Precedence: the shell environment always wins, so a one-off override still works:
#     RESERVE_DELAY_MS=25 scripts/run-suite.sh
# and the value recorded in each run's meta.json is whatever actually reached k6.
#
# That is what the `VAR="${VAR:-value}"` form below is for — keep it when you uncomment a
# line. A plain `VAR=value` would win over the command line instead, which is the opposite
# of what a defaults file should do and is easy to miss, because the run still succeeds and
# only the numbers are wrong.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- workload shape
# RESERVE_DELAY_MS — artificial per-reserve sleep inside the aggregate, standing in for
# expensive domain logic. The domain-work cost axis.
#
# Implemented on ALL EIGHT branches since 2026-08-06. TO stores it in reserve_delay_ms and
# sleeps in reserve(); ES replays it into aggregate state and sleeps in the @CommandHandler,
# so a replay or snapshot load does not pay it. Both charge it only once the reserve is known
# to succeed — on the out-of-stock path the rejection rate would otherwise become a hidden
# throughput lever.
#
# run-suite.sh warns if it is ever set for a variant that lacks the capability; variants.env
# is the source of truth. That guard matters because the failure would be silent — k6 sends
# the field to every branch, Spring ignores unknown properties, and meta.json records the
# requested value rather than what the server applied.
#
# Cost model: the sleep is held while the DB row lock (TO) or the pessimistic aggregate lock
# (ES) is held, so it caps throughput hard — roughly workers / (ITEMS_PER_ORDER x delay) on
# TO and DISTINCT_ITEMS / delay on ES. Sweep 5-50 ms before anything larger, and lower
# RATE / STEP_START to match or the staircase saturates at step 0 and reads INVALID.
#RESERVE_DELAY_MS="${RESERVE_DELAY_MS:-0}"

# DISTINCT_ITEMS — number of item aggregates; the contention axis. 1 is the hot-key sweep.
#DISTINCT_ITEMS="${DISTINCT_ITEMS:-6}"

# ITEMS_PER_ORDER — lines per order. Must be <= DISTINCT_ITEMS; the harness refuses otherwise.
# On ES this is also the saga round-trip count, since lines are reserved strictly sequentially.
#ITEMS_PER_ORDER="${ITEMS_PER_ORDER:-4}"

# PAYLOAD_BYTES — aggregate padding. Rides only on InventoryCreatedEvent, so it does NOT
# inflate appends; it inflates snapshot rows and the per-command Jackson deep copy. A
# copy-on-write cost lever. Note ~1 MiB of repeated bytes TOASTs down to ~12 kB on disk.
#PAYLOAD_BYTES="${PAYLOAD_BYTES:-0}"

#QTY_PER_LINE="${QTY_PER_LINE:-1}"
#SEED_QTY="${SEED_QTY:-2000000000}"
#ALLOW_DUP_LINES="${ALLOW_DUP_LINES:-false}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- load profile
#RATE="${RATE:-50}"
#DURATION="${DURATION:-10m}"
#WARMUP_ITERATIONS="${WARMUP_ITERATIONS:-5000}"
# Orders/s the warmup is delivered at. NO default — a run without one aborts at k6 init.
# points.env sets it per point; set it here (or per invocation) only for runs that name no
# POINT. Keep it below the slowest variant's sustained rate at the workload being run, or
# the warmup hands the measured window a backlog instead of a warm system.
#WARMUP_RATE="${WARMUP_RATE:-100}"

# capacity scenario only
#STEP_START="${STEP_START:-20}"
#STEP_INC="${STEP_INC:-20}"
#STEP_COUNT="${STEP_COUNT:-8}"
#STEP_PLATEAU_S="${STEP_PLATEAU_S:-120}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- read load
#READ_RATE="${READ_RATE:-0}"
#READ_MODE="${READ_MODE:-item}"
#READ_PAGE_SIZE="${READ_PAGE_SIZE:-100}"
